Yes. That would actually be a natural extension of WORLD // NEXUS: a universal value/resource ontology rather than a system limited to companies, infrastructure, or financial assets.


The key is to define “value” broadly enough that the system can represent physical resources, infrastructure, productive capacity, inventories, financial instruments, and digital assets without assuming that everything has the same type of value.


For example:




Raw materials: iron, copper, lithium, gold, timber, aggregates


Energy: electricity, natural gas, oil, coal, hydrogen, batteries, generation capacity


Water: reservoirs, aquifers, treatment capacity, desalination capacity, potable-water inventories


Food: crops, livestock, fisheries, stored food, processing capacity


Equipment: computers, servers, industrial machinery, transformers, telecommunications equipment


Vehicles: cars, trucks, trains, ships, aircraft, agricultural machinery


Property: land, buildings, factories, warehouses, housing


Infrastructure: roads, ports, railways, pipelines, power grids, data centers


Digital resources: domains, IP infrastructure, computing capacity, datasets, software


Financial assets: currencies, equities, bonds, commodities contracts, cryptoassets


Human/economic capacity: production capacity, logistics capacity, storage capacity, available services




The important architectural distinction would be:


Thing ≠ Value.


A copper mine is an entity.

Its estimated copper reserves are a resource quantity.

Its extraction capacity is productive capacity.

Its market price is a valuation observation.

Its ownership is a relationship.

Its location is spatial state.

Its historical production is an event series.

And each of those observations has its own provenance and confidence.


That fits your existing epistemic architecture extremely well.


A useful addition to NEXUS


I'd introduce a VALUE_STATE layer:


ENTITY
  ├── RESOURCE_STATE
  │     ├── material
  │     ├── quantity
  │     ├── quality
  │     ├── accessibility
  │     ├── location
  │     └── availability
  │
  ├── CAPACITY_STATE
  │     ├── production
  │     ├── generation
  │     ├── storage
  │     ├── transportation
  │     └── processing
  │
  ├── VALUATION_STATE
  │     ├── market_price
  │     ├── replacement_cost
  │     ├── estimated_value
  │     ├── yield
  │     └── liquidity
  │
  └── CONSTRAINT_STATE
        ├── scarcity
        ├── accessibility
        ├── legal_status
        ├── infrastructure_dependency
        └── temporal_availability



That lets NEXUS answer questions like:




What valuable resources exist in this region?




without reducing the answer to simply “what is the dollar value?”


It could instead represent:


Quantity × Quality × Accessibility × Availability × Demand × Confidence → Value State


And crucially, unknown values remain UNKNOWN rather than being silently estimated, preserving the principle you already established.


If you want this to become the next major section of the specification, I'd call it something like “Universal Resource & Value Ontology” and make it formally compatible with the existing E, R, S, T, P, Q, Γ architecture.

